The Lorneville Small Craft Harbour (SCH) is situated on a natural promontory extending northward into Lorneville Harbour.&nbsp; The harbour is further protected by a 165 m-long rubblemound breakwater (SCH Facility Number (#) 301) that extends out from the headland.&nbsp; The existing public wharf (#401) is a tee-shaped timber pile structure located on the lee side of the breakwater.&nbsp; The structure consists of square-post trestle bents along the approach stem and a timber pile bent system at the tee section.&nbsp; The wharf is more than 60 years old and has reached the end of its service life.

&nbsp;

The proposed works will involve the complete demolition and removal of the wharf structure.&nbsp; Demolition activities will likely be undertaken using land-based construction methods, either from a temporary earthen platform constructed along the interior side of the existing breakwater (#301) or by installation of a temporary trestle bridge.&nbsp; These approaches will facilitate the use of land-based equipment, including long-reach excavators, cranes, and haul trucks operating in dry conditions.&nbsp; Alternatively, the methodology could also involve a barge operation with equipment such as a crane and/or excavator operating from the floating platform to dismantle the wharf and relay material to the upland.

&nbsp;

The existing structure will likely be dismantled in sections or segments and temporarily placed along the approach road for further processing and separation.&nbsp; Materials suitable for recycling or reuse will be salvaged where practical, while unsuitable materials will be managed as waste, loaded onto trucks, and transported to provincially-approved disposal facilities.&nbsp; Demolition activities will also involve the removal of ballast boxes and stone and may also require incidental excavation of underwater sediments.&nbsp; Following completion of demolition activities, all temporary work areas and disturbed ground surfaces will be stabilized as required, with ballast and earthen materials/rock from temporary access reworked into the existing breakwater.&nbsp; All construction equipment and construction waste materials will be removed from the site.

&nbsp;

Demolition activities are anticipated to commence in November 2026 and be completed by the end of March 2027.&nbsp; Project commencement is, however, subject to DFO-SCH operational priorities and funding, as well as the completion of regulatory approval processes, therefore work may be delayed.&nbsp; The scope of the environmental effects determination (subject to S.82 of the Impact Assessment Act) will, therefore, be based on the works being completed over a period of three (3) months commencing within a period of 5 years following the determination of significance of environmental effects.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Lorneville SCH is a fishing harbour that was originally developed to serve the fishing industry in the area.&nbsp; The site has been used as a fishing harbour for several decades. Departmental records indicate that a breakwater has been in place since at least the late 1800s.&nbsp; Its well-protected location provides easy access to the fishing grounds of the Bay of Fundy.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Given that the existing wharf is well beyond its service life and load restricted, the status quo of leaving the deteriorated structures available and in service represents a contingent liability to the Crown.&nbsp; Repairs are not practical and complete reconstruction would be necessary to provide the same level of service.

&nbsp;

The four (4) homeport vessels at the Lorneville SCH have relocated to the Five Fathom Harbour SCH (approximately 8.5 kms to the northwest) due to the harbour&#39;s deteriorated condition.&nbsp; Following review of the costs associated with reconstruction of the wharf at Lorneville, DFO-SCH decided that permanent accommodation of these users at Five Fathom Harbour SCH through the installation of an additional floating dock was the most practicable and cost-effective long-term option.

k?ik?????m (Kwikwetlem First Nation) are proposing to restore the hydraulic connection between the Reeve Slough and the Coquitlam River by constructing new inlet and outlet channels and excavating a low gradient pilot channel through Reeve Slough to reestablish downstream flows. The Project will involve dredging and regrading the&nbsp;Reeve Slough, construction of a new inlet and outlet channel, installation of aquatic and riparian habitat features, and replacement of a non-functioning culvert.&nbsp;The Project will be designed and constructed with an in-kind partnership with Fisheries and Oceans Canada (DFO).

&nbsp;

The proponent has applied to Environment and Climate Change Canada for a Species at Risk Act permit under section 73(3).

Crystal Creek Homes is proposing to construct Black Birch Heights - a residential development located at Taza Park on Tsuut&#39;ina Nation reserve lands. The approximately 11632 square metre development consists of two six storey buildings containing in total 345 units atop a single level parkade.&nbsp;

The lot for the proposed project is located on an already cleared and serviced and will therefore tie into existing water, storm, and sanitary services, use Tsuut&#39;ina Nation public roadways and tie into electrical infrastructure grid.

Indigenous Services Canada is required to issue subleases under&nbsp;Section 53(1) of the Indian Act. The Project is not in the vicinity of any known species at risk or waterbodies.

Nakoda Oil and Gas Inc. is proposing to expand operations at the existing Goodstoney Aggregate Pit. It is proposed that that pit will expand from its permitted 10 hectares operation of 200,000 cubic metres of extraction to a total operational area of 39.9 hectares and an estimated 3,345,104 cubic meters of recoverable extraction through 5 phases of development. The project is located on Stoney Reserves 142, 143, 144&nbsp; south of HWY 1A near the community of&nbsp;M&icirc;n&icirc; Thn&icirc;.&nbsp;

The project activities will include site preparation, aggregate extraction, gravel crushing and washing, asphalt crushing, and reclamation. Topsoil will be salvaged and stored separately from other materials for use in reclamation. Reclamation will be progressive where compatible with operations. No species listed on schedule 1 of the Species at Risk Act have been identified within the project study area and the project overlaps with 0.17 of graminoid marsh wetland. The Bow river is 700m from the pit boundary and there are no waterbodies or watercourses within the proposed footprint.&nbsp;

Indigenous Services Canada will be required to issue a permit for the project under the&nbsp;Indian Act.&nbsp;

Ralmax Properties Limited is proposing to install a new barge ramp facility adjacent to 343 Bay Street in Victoria, B.C. Activities associated with the project include dredging and the installation of piles, riprap, winches, and a steel barge ramp.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Transport Canada (TC) is reviewing the project for approval under&nbsp;the&nbsp;Public Ports and Public Port Facilities Regulations&nbsp;(PPPPFR), as the proposed project falls within lands administered by Transport Canada. TC will also be issuing a licence and amending an existing lease to enable the work. Under the Impact Assessment Act, TC is reviewing project activities that relate to physical works on federal lands, specifically&nbsp;riprap installation and dredging related to the riprap installation.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The purpose of the riprap is to provide slope stability and protection at the base of the barge ramp. Dredging of seabed sediments would take place using a crane and clamshell bucket from a barge. Dredged materials would be transported to an upland facility for disposal. Following dredging, approximately 100 m3&nbsp;of riprap will be placed on federal lands.

Vancouver Airport Authority is proposing to construct and operate three temporary construction staging sites that may be used for soil stockpiling and/or construction equipment storage and staging.&nbsp; The three areas are located on Ferguson Road on the northside of the Vancouver International Airport in Richmond.

&nbsp;

The Proposed Project

&nbsp;

The proposed YVR North Construction Yards project will involve clearing of vegetation and setup of level staging areas to stockpile soil, sort construction materials and park construction equipment. Site preparation will include vegetation clearing, removal of soil, grading, excavation, placement of gravel, construction of access driveways and installation of fencing and signage.

&nbsp;

Operation of the construction yards will involve construction equipment parking and operation, soil import, soil sorting, soil placement, bulk aggregate storage, grading, excavation and site restoration.

This project to repair the sanitary sewer system in Timiskaming consists of several components.&nbsp;

- Installation of a storm-water system to carry away stormwater runoff and water from building foundation drains;

- Reconstruction of existing streets to address current issues with inadequate surface drainage in the community;

- Construction of a new storm-water outfall that will discharge into the stream on the other side of Stranger Road.&nbsp;

Lubicon Lake Band is proposing the construction of a photovoltaic solar array in a green field development. This project is a sub-project of a larger Lubicon Lake Band Community Infrastructure Project.

This project will include the following components:
- Geotechnical investigation of the array area;
- Tree clearing;
- Site grading;
- Construction of the solar array; and
- Construction of an access road.&nbsp;

The project site is located north of and immediately adjacent to Highway 986. There is an existing cell tower site and access road to the west of the project site. An existing power pole (ATCO Feeder #812-502) is south of the project site on the south side of Highway 986.


New infrastructure associated with the solar array will include solar module frames, a 600 V e-house, transformers, field junction boxes, direct buried cable, and power poles. A new access road will be constructed to connect the array area with the existing access road to the west.&nbsp;


Construction will include tree clearing, site grading, and trenching. Routine maintenance will be required after construction is complete which includes truck access to the e-house and solar panels. Grass will be cut within the fenced perimeter to maintain appropriate access to the panels.

Natural Resources Canada and Indigenous Service Canada are providing funding for the Project.

Project Objective

Growing electricity needs in the Orl&eacute;ans area identified in 2022 by the Greater Ottawa Regional Planning Working Group mean that a new 230 kilovolt (kV) electricity transmission supply is required. To meet the current and future needs of the community, Hydro One Networks Inc. (Hydro One) plans to convert an existing 115 kV circuit to 230 kV and install a new 115 kV circuit within an existing transmission corridor between Hydro One&#39;s Hawthorne Transformer Station (TS) and Orl&eacute;ans TS in Ottawa, Ontario.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Federal and Provincial Involvement 

The project crosses approximately 12 kilometres in Ottawa, about 6.4 kilometres of which is property owned by the National Capital Commission (NCC) within the National Capital Greenbelt. The portion of the project on NCC property is subject to a Federal Land Use, Design, and Transaction Approval and an Environmental Effects Evaluation under Section 82 of the Impact Assessment Act.

&nbsp;

The overall project is also subject to the provincial Class Environmental Assessment for Transmission Facilities, under which Hydro One has undertaken engagement with Indigenous communities, government agencies, stakeholders, and the public, including through Community Open Houses.&nbsp; Additionally, the project must receive Leave to Construct approval from the Ontario Energy Board.

&nbsp;

Scope of Work 

To replace the 115 kV circuit, a new approximately 12-kilometre 115 kV pole transmission line (wood, composite, or steel) is planned to be constructed in the middle of an existing hydro corridor, between two existing transmission lines. Most of the new line will consist of wood or composite poles in an &quot;H-Frame&quot; configuration. Monopole and lattice steel structures will be required at the Highway 417 crossing, and for station egress and entrances.

&nbsp;

The existing circuit already has the appropriate conductor and insulators to operate at 230 kV along its length

&nbsp;

Permanent infrastructure on NCC lands will include one steel monopole occupying approximately 11 m&sup2;, three lattice structures occupying approximately 12 m&sup2; each, one three-pole structure occupying approximately 80 m&sup2; including guy-wire anchors, and 38 H-frame structures occupying a combined area of approximately 76 m&sup2;. The project will also require replacement of one culvert and installation of one new watercourse crossing.

&nbsp;

Environmental Setting&nbsp; 

The project crosses wetlands, including the Mer Bleue Bog, watercourses, grasslands, agricultural fields, and small portions of wooded areas and is within an existing transmission corridor.

&nbsp;

Locating the project within an existing transmission corridor substantially reduces potential environmental and socio-economic impacts, including vegetation removal, habitat fragmentation, property acquisition requirements, and associated project costs.

&nbsp;

Hydro One has worked collaboratively with the project&#39;s design engineer and the NCC to minimize the footprint of the project in environmentally sensitive areas including Mer Bleue.

&nbsp;

Construction Timing and Activities 

Construction is planned to start in early 2027 and will be complete by the end of 2029.

&nbsp;

The construction area will be accessed with small bulldozers, tri-axle dump trucks, and excavators to install access routes and work pads.&nbsp; Specialized machinery designed to augur holes, erect structures, and string conductors will be used to install the transmission line.

&nbsp;

Existing access routes will be used as much as possible to reach each structure location.&nbsp; New access routes consisting of gravel overlaying geotextile membrane will be required in some areas. Work pads about 18 m x 18 m square for pole structures and about 30 m x 30 m square for lattice structures will be required, along with about six approximately 30 m x 60 m stringing pads. Work pads and access routes in agricultural areas will be removed following construction.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Temporary trail closures within and near the corridor may be required during construction.

&nbsp;

Approximately 1.1 ha of trees would need to be removed from NCC property to ensure the project meets line clearance requirements. Areas requiring tree removal will have stumps removed and will be seeded with a pollinator-friendly seed mix. Tree removals will be compensated for as per NCC&#39;s advice in accordance with the NCC&#39;s Forest Strategy.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

More Information 

For more information, please consult Hydro One&#39;s public website for the project: https://www.hydroone.com/about/corporate-information/major-projects/hawthorne-to-orleans

The National Capital Commission (NCC) is acquiring an 8.9-hectare parcel of land located adjacent to Gatineau Park at 241 Boulevard de la Cit&eacute;-des-Jeunes in Gatineau (the &quot;Asticou Centre&quot;). The NCC proposes to undertake the demolition of nine (9) existing institutional buildings located on this parcel of land for the purpose of eventually re-naturalizing the site.

&nbsp;

This project supports several NCC policies and long-term land management objectives, particularly those related to the stewardship of federal lands, the protection and restoration of natural environments. This project advances NCC&#39;s broader goals for sustainable land use, landscape restoration, and the responsible planning of federal real property in the National Capital Region.

&nbsp;

The proposed project would take place on National Capital Commission (NCC) lands and approval under the Federal Land Use, Design and Transaction Approval (FLUDTA) process is required for the demolition of the existing buildings.

&nbsp;

The scope of work will include but may not be limited to the following: &nbsp;


	abatement of hazardous construction substances;
	removing the buildings;
	removing subsurface structures;
	backfilling the building footprints.


&nbsp;

The buildings that are scheduled for removal are Blocks&nbsp;100, 200, 300, 400, 600, 700, 800, 900, and 1000. These buildings are single storey structures with one (1) basement level. Block 500 and associated existing parking lots will be retained.

&nbsp;

All buildings have undergone detailed hazardous materials surveys. Hazardous construction materials will be removed and disposed of in accordance with provincial and federal requirements prior to building demolition. Subsurface structures, including foundations, basement slabs, underground utilities, and other buried infrastructure will also be removed. Soil quality has been assessed and determined to be non-hazardous.

&nbsp;

Heritage mitigation obligations associated with the project included detailed photographic documentation and a virtual tour of the buildings, and these were accepted by the Federal Heritage Buildings Review Office (FHBRO).

&nbsp;

The 8.9-hectare parcel of the Asticou Centre being acquired by the NCC is located immediately east of Gatineau Park&#39;s wooded lands. The overall Asticou Centre site contains or is adjacent to several ecological features, including a permanent fish-bearing water course, intermittent watercourses and associated wetlands. These hydrological features are outside the planned demolition work area, but measures will be implemented to prevent possible impacts from erosion and sediment.

&nbsp;

There are species of conservation interest in the general area such as wood thrush, bats, snapping and painted turtles. Measures to exclude these species from the demolition work area and buildings will be implemented prior to nesting seasons. An inspection by a qualified biologist will be completed after the installation of the exclusion measures but prior to the start of the work. Work will be required to stop in zones where species are identified pending instructions and recommendations from a qualified biologist.

&nbsp;

The work will rely on standard construction equipment, including excavators, loaders, haul trucks, and compaction equipment. The demolition is expected to begin in mid- to late 2026 with completion in Spring 2028. Best management practices will be applied for noise, dust, sediment control, and site security. No tree removals are anticipated, and a 90&nbsp;percent waste diversion target will be pursued through material recycling and recovery.

&nbsp;

Once cleared and long-term restoration is completed, the site will contribute to improved ecological connectivity with Gatineau Park. The parking lot (P-8) used by the public to access Gatineau Park is expected to remain accessible throughout this work.
